.TH FLEET-BOARD 1 "2026-11-03" "cti-agent 1.4" "CTI Fleet Manual"
.SH NAME
fleet-board \- the fleet's append-only message board: post, read, prune and tail board.md
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fleet\-board post
.I from to kind body...
.br
.B fleet\-board say
.I to kind body...
.br
.B fleet\-board read
.RI [ handle ]
.br
.B fleet\-board resolve
.I id
.br
.B fleet\-board expire
.I days
.br
.B fleet\-board prune
.I pattern
.br
.B fleet\-board tail
.RI [ n ]
.br
.B fleet\-board
.PP
.B sudo cti\-agent fleet\-board
.I command
.RI [ arguments ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fleet\-board
is how the lanes, the orchestrator and the operator talk to each other.
The board is one text file,
.IR $FLEET_HOME/board.md ,
with one message per line:
.PP
.EX
[2026\-10\-12 09:30] @enrich  \-> @you     Q        id=q17 NVD rate\-limited
.EE
.PP
The runners post their results and failures to it
.RB ( run\-digest
posts
.B INFO
when a digest is sent and
.B ERROR
when a run fails), the orchestrator heartbeat reads it every beat (its tool grant is
.BR say ,
.BR read ,
.BR tail ,
.B resolve
and
.BR expire ;
not
.B post
or
.BR prune ), and the operator answers on it by posting as
.BR @operator .
Handles in use are
.B @you
(the orchestrator),
.BR @operator ,
.BR @ingest ,
.BR @enrich ,
.BR @scout ,
.BR @brief ,
.BR @patchtuesday ,
.BR @mailbox ,
.BR @appscan ,
.B @domains
and
.BR @all ;
they are a convention: any well-formed
.B @name
is accepted.
.PP
Writes
.RB ( post ,
.BR prune )
are serialised with a lock directory,
.IR $FLEET_HOME/.board.lock ,
created with
.BR mkdir (1),
which is atomic, so two writers never interleave. A lock older than 60
seconds is taken to belong to a dead process and is removed. The lock is
released when the command exits. Reads do not lock.
.PP
Every invocation, including a bad one, first creates
.I $FLEET_HOME/archive
and touches the board and the archive file.
.PP
.B fleet\-board
sends no mail; reaching the operator by email is
.BR cti\-alert 's
job.
.PP
Inside a heartbeat (when
.B CHECKIN_BEAT_ID
is set by
.BR run\-checkin (1))
what \fBread\fR and \fBtail\fR print output is passed through
.BR fleet\-sanitize (1):
control characters, bidirectional and zero\-width characters are removed and
over\-long lines are cut with a
.B [cut N chars]
marker. Outside a heartbeat the output is untouched.
.SH COMMANDS
With no command,
.B read
is run.
.TP
.BI post " from to kind body..."
Append one line: the local time to the minute, then
.IR from ,
.I to
and
.I kind
each padded to 8 characters, then the body. The remaining arguments are
joined into the body; line breaks (newline and carriage return) in it become
spaces and runs of spaces collapse to one, because a message is always one
line.
.I from
and
.I to
must be
.B @
followed by letters, digits,
.BR . ,
.B _
or
.BR \- ,
at most 32 characters;
.I kind
must be upper-case letters and digits, at most 16. Anything else is refused
with exit status 2 and nothing is written: a newline in a handle used to write
a second, forged line that every reader trusts. The fleet uses the kinds
.BR Q ,
.BR A ,
.BR INFO ,
.BR P1 ,
.B APPROVE
and
.BR ERROR .
An empty body is refused.
.TP
.BR read " [\fIhandle\fR]"
Print every line addressed to
.I handle
or to
.BR @all ,
that is, lines matching
.BI "\-> +(" handle "|@all) "
as an extended regular expression; the handle is not escaped.
Default:\~\fB@you\fR. Prints
.BI "(nothing for " handle )
when nothing matches.
.TP
.BI say " to kind body..."
The same as
.B post
with the sender fixed as
.BR @you .
This is the orchestrator heartbeat's way to post: it is granted
.B say
and not
.BR post ,
so it can never sign a line as
.B @operator
(an answer the next beat would act on) or
.BR @systemd .
.TP
.BI resolve " id"
Archive the line carrying
.BI id= id
and every line carrying
.BI re: id\fR,
matched as whole words, not as a pattern: resolving
.B q17
leaves
.B re:q170
alone. The id is letters, digits,
.BR . ,
.B _
and
.BR \- ,
starting with a letter or digit. Prints the number of lines archived.
.TP
.BI expire " days"
Archive every line whose timestamp is more than
.I days
old, 2 to 365.
.B ERROR
lines are kept for 7 days whatever
.I days
is, so an alert cannot be aged out before the operator has had a chance to see
it. Lines without a timestamp are kept. Prints the number of lines archived.
.TP
.BI prune " pattern"
Move every line matching the extended regular expression
.I pattern
to the end of
.I $FLEET_HOME/archive/board\-archive.md
and remove it from the board. Prints
.BI "pruned lines matching: " pattern\fR.
The board is rewritten through a temporary file, so afterwards it has mode
0600 and is owned by whoever ran the prune. The orchestrator's heartbeat is not granted
.BR prune ,
since
.B prune .
would empty the board, ERROR lines included; it prunes with
.B resolve
and
.B expire
instead. The operator runs
.BR prune .
.TP
.BR tail " [\fIn\fR]"
The last
.I n
lines of the board.
Default:\~\fB20\fR.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.B fleet\-board
does not read
.I fleet.env
itself.
.TP
.B FLEET_HOME
Where the board, the archive and the lock live.
Default:\~\fI$HOME/fleet\fR. Set to
.I /var/lib/cti\-agent
by the systemd units and
.BR cti\-agent (1).
.TP
.B LOCK_TIMEOUT
Seconds to wait for the lock before giving up, checked in 0.2-second steps.
Default:\~\fB10\fR.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/lib/cti\-agent/board.md
The board.
.TP
.I /var/lib/cti\-agent/archive/board\-archive.md
Pruned lines, appended in board order.
.TP
.I /var/lib/cti\-agent/.board.lock
The lock directory, present only while a write is in progress. One left by
a killed process is removed by the next writer after 60 seconds.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including a
.B read
that found nothing.
.TP
.B 1
.B post
without
.IR from ,
.I to
or
.IR kind ,
or with an empty body;
.B prune
without a pattern; or the lock could not be taken within
.B LOCK_TIMEOUT
seconds.
.TP
.B 2
Unknown command; a usage line is printed. Also
.B post
with a
.I from
or
.I to
that is not an
.B @name
or a
.I kind
that is not upper-case letters and digits, or any of them over its length
limit.
.SH EXAMPLES
The latest traffic, and what is waiting for the orchestrator:
.PP
.EX
sudo cti\-agent fleet\-board tail 30
sudo cti\-agent fleet\-board read @you
.EE
.PP
Answer a question the orchestrator escalated as
.BR q17 :
.PP
.EX
sudo cti\-agent fleet\-board post @operator @you A "re:q17 yes, go ahead"
.EE
.PP
What the operator has said:
.PP
.EX
sudo grep \-F '@operator \->' /var/lib/cti\-agent/board.md
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fleet\-db (1),
.BR run\-checkin (1),
.BR run\-digest (1),
.BR cti\-alert (1),
.BR cti\-agent (1)
.PP
fleet\-kit/fleet/CLAUDE.md, "Message board \- you are the postmaster";
RUNBOOK.md section 15.
